     1	# Exact zero witnesses for linear tropical valued CSPs
     2	
     3	| profile field | value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| native domain | optimization |
     7	| object regime | continuous analytic |
     8	| computational core | linear algebra |
     9	| certificate form | exact symbolic rational vector |
    10	| intended intuition | invariant — paired dense rows have a one-coordinate difference |
    11	| domain essentiality | native |
    12	| reduction | none |
    13	
    14	This generator uses Fujii, Iwamasa, and Kimura, [“Quantaloidal Approach to
    15	Constraint Satisfaction”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.01778). It hands the solver
    16	a linear tropical valued CSP over the reals: finitely many rational affine
    17	residuals and the objective `F(s) = max_e |a_e·s-b_e|`. The required witness is
    18	a bounded rational assignment with `F(s)=0`. Verification substitutes the
    19	assignment into every form using exact rational arithmetic. Since `F` is
    20	nonnegative, zero also certifies global optimality.
    21	
    22	Trust status: all local gates G1–G9(c) pass. The required STEP 4 oracle evidence
    23	is **blocked, not passed**: the supplied OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key
    24	limit exceeded” before one completed attempt. The retained transcript contains
    25	only those script-owned errors, and no hinted or placebo transcript exists.
    26	
    27	## Family and construction
    28	
    29	Section 5 defines a tropical valued CSP by
    30	`Sol(I)(s) = sup(rho(s(x))-sigma(x))` and asks for its infimum. Section 5.2
    31	specializes this to linear real-valued relations, where `Sol(I)` is a maximum
    32	of affine functions. Each displayed equation in this module abbreviates the
    33	two paper-native forms `a·s-b` and `-a·s+b`.
    34	
    35	Generation is inverse. It first samples pairwise-distinct bounded rationals
    36	`s_j`, then samples integer rows and sets every right-hand side to `a·s`.
    37	Among paired rows, one pair changes only coordinate `j` for every `j`; subtracting
    38	that pair proves and recovers `s_j`. Other pairs change two coordinates and are
    39	near misses. Their individual rows use the same coefficient distribution as
    40	the certificate-bearing pairs. The generator therefore knows the witness
    41	before it creates the instance and never solves its output.
    42	
    43	## Why Track B
    44	
    45	This is explicitly not a Track A claim. Section 5.2 reduces linear TVCSP
    46	minimization to linear programming, hence gives a polynomial-time mechanical
    47	method. For the zero-level system used here, the implemented exact reference is
    48	fraction-free Bareiss elimination: `O(m n^2)` arithmetic for `m` equations and
    49	`n` variables. At hard it solved 8/8, used 1,406,272 counted exact operations,
    50	and took at most 0.844 seconds in the final self-test.
    51	
    52	After recognizing the invariant, each of 64 coordinates needs one RHS
    53	subtraction, one coefficient subtraction, and one exact division: 192 exact
    54	operations. The benchmark is the gap between scanning a large dense system and
    55	noticing that positionwise row pairs isolate coordinates. The paper’s easy
    56	regime is not hidden: Section 5.1 gives the finite-domain Siggers dichotomy, and
    57	Section 5.2 says the continuous linear case here is in P.
    58	
    59	## Worked demo
    60	
    61	For the `demo` preset and seed 0, the complete rendered instance is:
    62	
    63	```text
    64	Linear tropical valued CSP over the real numbers
    65	
    66	There are 3 real variables s_0,...,s_2.
    67	For every displayed equation e, let
    68	  r_e(s) = a_e,0*s_0 + ... + a_e,2*s_2 - b_e.
    69	The tropical minimax objective is
    70	  F(s) = max_e max(r_e(s), -r_e(s)) = max_e |r_e(s)|.
    71	This is the maximum of finitely many rational affine forms, so F(s) >= 0.
    72	The instance promises one unique vector in the bounded language below with F(s)=0.
    73	Find that vector.  Reaching zero also certifies global optimality because F is nonnegative.
    74	
    75	Each equation line has the exact format
    76	  index : b | a_0 a_1 ... a_2
    77	and means sum_j a_j*s_j = b.  Indices and variables are 0-based.
    78	All coefficients are integers and every b is an exact reduced rational.
    79	Equation order does not change F.
    80	
    81	n = 3; equations = 8
    82	certificate bounds: 0 < |numerator| <= 4, denominator <= 2, entries pairwise distinct
    83	
    84	EQUATIONS
    85	0 : -7/2 | 1 -2 -1
    86	1 : -29/2 | 3 -3 1
    87	2 : -7/2 | 1 2 3
    88	3 : 37/2 | -3 2 -3
    89	4 : -23/2 | 1 -2 1
    90	5 : 3/2 | 3 1 1
    91	6 : -7/2 | 1 -2 -1
    92	7 : 41/2 | 1 2 -3
    93	END EQUATIONS
    94	
    95	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as exactly 3 comma-separated
    96	reduced rationals in variable order s_0,...,s_2.  Write an integer q as q and
    97	a nonintegral rational as num/den with a positive denominator.
    98	Syntax example: <answer>1/2, -3, 4/5</answer>
    99	Output nothing else inside the tags.
   100	```
