     1	# Exact zero witnesses for linear tropical valued CSPs
     2	
     3	| profile field | value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| native domain | optimization |
     7	| object regime | continuous analytic |
     8	| computational core | linear algebra |
     9	| certificate form | rational vector |
    10	| intended intuition | invariant — paired dense rows have a one-coordinate difference |
    11	| domain essentiality | native |
    12	| reduction | none |
    13	
    14	This generator uses Fujii, Iwamasa, and Kimura, [“Quantaloidal Approach to
    15	Constraint Satisfaction”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.01778). It hands the solver
    16	a linear tropical valued CSP over the reals: finitely many rational affine
    17	residuals and the objective `F(s) = max_e |a_e·s-b_e|`. The required witness is
    18	a bounded rational assignment with `F(s)=0`. Verification substitutes the
    19	assignment into every form using exact rational arithmetic. Since `F` is
    20	nonnegative, zero also certifies global optimality.
    21	
    22	Trust status: all local gates G1–G9(c) pass. The required STEP 4 oracle evidence
    23	is **incomplete, not passed**. The funded key completed eight calls: `easy` and
    24	`medium` were solved 3/3, while two `hard` attempts failed. It then returned HTTP
    25	403 “Key limit exceeded” four times before the third `hard` attempt, so the
    26	harness correctly refused to issue a verdict. The structural and placebo arms
    27	are authentic `harden.py` outputs but are error-only and supply no evidence.
    28	
    29	## Family and construction
    30	
    31	Section 5 defines a tropical valued CSP by
    32	`Sol(I)(s) = sup(rho(s(x))-sigma(x))` and asks for its infimum. Section 5.2
    33	specializes this to linear real-valued relations, where `Sol(I)` is a maximum
    34	of affine functions. Each displayed equation in this module abbreviates the
    35	two paper-native forms `a·s-b` and `-a·s+b`.
    36	
    37	Generation is inverse. It first samples pairwise-distinct bounded rationals
    38	`s_j`, then samples integer rows and sets every right-hand side to `a·s`.
    39	Among paired rows, one pair changes only coordinate `j` for every `j`; subtracting
    40	that pair proves and recovers `s_j`. Other pairs change two coordinates and are
    41	near misses. Base rows and coefficient replacements use the same sampling rules
    42	in both pair types; the number of changed coordinates is the structural signal.
    43	The generator therefore knows the witness
    44	before it creates the instance and never solves its output.
    45	
    46	## Why Track B
    47	
    48	This is explicitly not a Track A claim. Proposition 5.2 and Theorem 5.4 give the
    49	finite-domain CSP/TVCSP classification, while Section 5.2 reduces linear TVCSP
    50	minimization to linear programming, hence gives a polynomial-time mechanical
    51	method. For the zero-level system used here, the implemented exact reference is
    52	fraction-free Bareiss elimination: `O(m n^2)` arithmetic for `m` equations and
    53	`n` variables. At hard it solved 8/8, used 1,406,272 counted exact operations,
    54	and took at most 4.492 wall-clock seconds in the final, heavily contended shared-
    55	runner self-test.
    56	
    57	The prior-triage suggestion of a planted finite CSP was not used: Theorem 5.4 is
    58	a worst-case dichotomy and gives no distributional-hardness guarantee for a
    59	planted satisfiable distribution. Section 5.2 instead licenses the explicit
    60	Track B claim made here.
    61	
    62	After recognizing the invariant, each of 64 coordinates needs one RHS
    63	subtraction, one coefficient subtraction, and one exact division: 192 exact
    64	operations. The benchmark is the gap between scanning a large dense system and
    65	noticing that positionwise row pairs isolate coordinates. The paper’s easy
    66	regime is not hidden: Section 5.1 gives the finite-domain Siggers dichotomy, and
    67	Section 5.2 says the continuous linear case here is in P.
    68	
    69	## Worked demo
    70	
    71	For the `demo` preset and seed 0, the complete rendered instance is:
    72	
    73	```text
    74	Linear tropical valued CSP over the real numbers
    75	
    76	There are 3 real variables s_0,...,s_2.
    77	For every displayed equation e, let
    78	  r_e(s) = a_e,0*s_0 + ... + a_e,2*s_2 - b_e.
    79	The tropical minimax objective is
    80	  F(s) = max_e max(r_e(s), -r_e(s)) = max_e |r_e(s)|.
    81	This is the maximum of finitely many rational affine forms, so F(s) >= 0.
    82	The instance promises one unique vector in the bounded language below with F(s)=0.
    83	Find that vector.  Reaching zero also certifies global optimality because F is nonnegative.
    84	
    85	Each equation line has the exact format
    86	  index : b | a_0 a_1 ... a_2
    87	and means sum_j a_j*s_j = b.  Indices and variables are 0-based.
    88	All coefficients are integers and every b is an exact reduced rational.
    89	Equation order does not change F.
    90	
    91	n = 3; equations = 8
    92	certificate bounds: 0 < |numerator| <= 4, denominator <= 2, entries pairwise distinct
    93	
    94	EQUATIONS
    95	0 : -7/2 | 1 -2 -1
    96	1 : -29/2 | 3 -3 1
    97	2 : -7/2 | 1 2 3
    98	3 : 37/2 | -3 2 -3
    99	4 : -23/2 | 1 -2 1
   100	5 : 3/2 | 3 1 1
   101	6 : -7/2 | 1 -2 -1
   102	7 : 41/2 | 1 2 -3
   103	END EQUATIONS
   104	
   105	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as exactly 3 comma-separated
   106	reduced rationals in variable order s_0,...,s_2.  Write an integer q as q and
   107	a nonintegral rational as num/den with a positive denominator.
   108	Syntax example: <answer>1/2, -3, 4/5</answer>
   109	Output nothing else inside the tags.
   110	```
   111	
   112	The answer is `<answer>1/2, 4, -4</answer>` and verifies as `(True, "ok")`.
   113	Replacing its first entry by `5` returns `(False, "entry 0 violates the declared
   114	numerator/denominator bounds")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by
   115	comparing rows 0–3 with rows 4–7 and using the three one-coordinate pairs.
